    lesson: 'THE TERM RISK IS APPLIED TO FOUR ENTIRELY DIFFERENT THINGS, and running them together is probably the largest single source of avoidable harm in this subject. They behave differently, arrive by different routes, and are prepared for by different means. A person who can say WHICH ONE they are carrying has already done most of the useful thinking, and a person who says only "it is risky" has said almost nothing at all. THE FIRST IS PERMANENT LOSS OF THE CAPITAL. The enterprise fails, the borrower cannot pay, the thing proves worth nothing. This is what the word conjures and it is entirely real. It is also, for most ordinary arrangements held by most ordinary households, NOT the one that does the damage — which matters, because a person braced wholly against this one is regularly ambushed by the other three. THE SECOND IS INACCESSIBILITY. Nothing has failed and the value is intact; the holding simply cannot be turned into spendable money at the moment it is wanted, because it is locked, or takes weeks, or can only be sold to a buyer who happens to want it that week and none is present. This is habitually described as safety and is indistinguishable from safety right up until the afternoon the money is needed. THE DISTINCTION BETWEEN SAFE AND REACHABLE IS ONE OF THE MOST EXPENSIVE DISTINCTIONS IN THIS BUSINESS, and one word is used for both by people who are not lying and are not being clear either. THE THIRD IS EROSION OF WHAT THE MONEY BUYS. The sum is present, it is reachable, and it commands fewer real goods than it did. This is the quiet one: the figure on the statement is unchanged or gently larger, a week of ordinary provisions costs more, and the pile does less work. NO LETTER ARRIVES TO ANNOUNCE IT. Nothing presents as having failed. It is nonetheless a real loss, and it falls most reliably on the most careful people, precisely because carefulness about the first risk so often means sitting still inside the third. THE FOURTH IS BEING FORCED TO SELL AT THE WORST MOMENT, and it deserves the most attention because it gets the least. ITS ORIGIN IS NOT THE INVESTMENT — IT IS THE HOUSEHOLD. The van dies, the job ends, illness arrives, a relative needs help. Money is needed this month, the only money available is in something that happens to be down, and it is sold down, turning a temporary movement into a permanent loss for reasons that had nothing whatever to do with the merits of the thing owned. NEARLY EVERY SERIOUS HARM THIS COURSE CAN HELP A READER AVOID RUNS THROUGH THIS FOURTH CATEGORY, and it is almost never on anybody list, because the attention is fixed on the investment while the danger is standing in the kitchen. NOTICE WHAT THAT IMPLIES, because it is the practical heart of this lesson: the most effective protection against the fourth risk is not a cleverer investment. It is money that is reachable, kept somewhere dull, deliberately not doing anything impressive — so that when the van dies, the van is paid for out of the dull money and the long thing is left alone to be long. That is not timidity. It is the only arrangement in which the long thing gets to work at all. Joseph neither abolishes the famine nor pretends he can. He names a specific danger, finds the interval before it, and stores against THAT: "And let them gather all the food of those good years that come, and lay up corn under the hand of Pharaoh, and let them keep food in the cities." (Genesis 41:35) SEE HOW CONCRETE IT IS — which food, under whose hand, kept where — and see what it is for: "And that food shall be for store to the land against the seven years of famine, which shall be in the land of Egypt; that the land perish not through the famine." (Genesis 41:36) Against a named thing, for a named outcome. A danger specified exactly can be prepared for exactly. A danger left as a general sense that things may go badly can only be worried about, and worry stores no corn.',
