    lesson: 'DISTRIBUTING WHAT YOU HOLD ACROSS SEVERAL UNRELATED THINGS REDUCES THE HARM FROM ANY SINGLE ONE OF THEM FAILING. That is the entire benefit and it is a real one. If everything is in one thing and that thing fails, the loss is total; if it is spread across eight and one fails, the loss is an eighth. The arithmetic is elementary and nobody contests it. NOW THE PART THAT IS ROUTINELY OMITTED, AND THE OMISSION IS NOT ACCIDENTAL. Spreading accomplishes nothing whatever against a common cause — the case where all of them fall together. If a whole economy has a bad year, holding eight things that all belong to that economy affords no protection at all, because whatever went wrong reached all eight. SPREADING PROTECTS AGAINST NOT KNOWING WHICH ONE. IT DOES NOT PROTECT AGAINST A THING THAT TOUCHES EVERYTHING. Those are different propositions, and the comfortable rendering of this advice consistently implies the second while delivering only the first. THIS IS A PATTERN RATHER THAN AN ACCIDENT AND IS WORTH NAMING AS ONE. If a person comes away from a conversation about this feeling SAFE, they have been sold something, because the practice does not produce safety and was never grounded in a claim that it did. What it produces is the condition of NOT BEING RUINED BY A SINGLE MISTAKE — genuinely valuable, well worth arranging, and categorically not the same thing. The distance between those two sentences is where most misplaced confidence in this subject is manufactured, usually with no intention to deceive at all, by people repeating a comfortable formulation that was handed to them. AND THE CONCENTRATION MOST HOUSEHOLDS ACTUALLY CARRY IS NOT AN INVESTMENT DECISION. Consider what a household depends on. For very many people the wage comes from one employer, the pension is entangled with that same employer, and sometimes a holding of that employer own shares sits beside them. On a list that presents as three separate items. IT IS ONE ITEM WITH THREE NAMES, and if that employer has a difficult year all three arrive in the same week — exactly the week the household is least able to absorb any of them. THIS IS THE COMMONEST CONCENTRATION IN ORDINARY LIFE, it is almost never counted, and it is not counted because it does not look like an investment decision. A person who notices it has learned something more useful than any instruction about how many holdings to own, and noticing it costs nothing and requires no product. Ecclesiastes gives the practice and its ground in one verse, and the ground is the honest part: "Give a portion to seven, and also to eight; for thou knowest not what evil shall be upon the earth." (Ecclesiastes 11:2) SEVEN, AND ALSO EIGHT — do not stop at the number that feels sufficient, because the feeling of sufficiency is itself a quiet confidence about the future. And the reason given is not confidence at all: FOR THOU KNOWEST NOT WHAT EVIL SHALL BE UPON THE EARTH. The practice rests on an admission that what is coming cannot be seen from here. THAT IS A COMPLETELY DIFFERENT FOUNDATION from the one usually laid under the same advice, and it sets the practice its proper limit in the same breath that it commends it. A person who spreads because they cannot see is being wise. A person who spreads and then believes they can see is standing exactly where they started, carrying the same blindness with a better feeling about it.',
