    lesson: 'THERE IS ONE QUESTION THIS ENTIRE INDUSTRY CANNOT ANSWER, and it is not a technical one. HOW MUCH IS ENOUGH? Every instrument, every platform, every piece of counsel and every confident voice is built to help a person obtain MORE, and not one of them contains anything whatever that says when to stop. That is not an accusation against anybody. IT IS WHAT THE MACHINERY IS FOR, and machinery has no opinion about destinations. But the consequence is serious: a person fitted out with the whole apparatus and no answer to this question has been equipped to run faster with no idea where the finish is. So the answer has to come from somewhere the apparatus does not reach, and the Word gives one that disappoints both of the parties expecting to be vindicated by it. IT DOES NOT COMMEND POVERTY. IT DOES NOT COMMEND RICHES. "Remove far from me vanity and lies: give me neither poverty nor riches; feed me with food convenient for me" (Proverbs 30:8). NEITHER END IS ASKED FOR. And the ground of the request is what repays the closest reading: "Lest I be full, and deny thee, and say, Who is the LORD? or lest I be poor, and steal, and take the name of my God in vain." (Proverbs 30:9) EXAMINE WHAT THAT REASON IS AND WHAT IT IS NOT. It is not that riches are precarious, and it is not that poverty builds character. BOTH ENDS ARE REFUSED FOR AN IDENTICAL REASON, AND THE REASON IS NOT FINANCIAL AT ALL: each has its own characteristic way of separating a person from Yahweh. Fullness produces denial — WHO IS THE LORD — the forgetting that comes precisely from not needing anything. Want produces theft, and the name taken in vain on top of it. The prayer is about the relationship all the way through, and the provision asked for is simply whatever preserves it. A reader who takes this passage as an endorsement of modest living has kept the conclusion and thrown away the argument, and the argument is the part that does the work. Paul comes at the same point from another direction, and he deliberately uses the very word the whole subject trades in: "But godliness with contentment is great gain. For we brought nothing into this world, and it is certain we can carry nothing out. And having food and raiment let us be therewith content." (1 Timothy 6:6, 7, 8) GREAT GAIN. He does not tell the reader that gain is illegitimate. HE RELOCATES IT, and he uses the commercial word on purpose so the relocation cannot be missed. AND NOW THE NECESSARY HONESTY, WITHOUT WHICH THIS LESSON TURNS INTO A WAY OF LOOKING DOWN ON PEOPLE. NOBODY HERE WILL TELL YOU WHAT YOUR NUMBER SHOULD BE. It is not the same for a single person and a household of six, not the same at twenty-five and at seventy, not the same for somebody supporting a dependent relative and somebody who is not. Anyone who hands out one figure for everybody has stopped thinking and started preaching, and this house will not do it. WHAT THIS LESSON ASKS IS ONLY THAT A NUMBER EXIST — because a person without one can never arrive, and a person who can never arrive keeps running long after the reason for running has gone, usually without noticing the moment it left. And then the one question that tells the truth about the person asking it: IF YOU HAD YOUR NUMBER TOMORROW, WOULD YOU STOP, OR WOULD THE NUMBER MOVE? A number that always moves is not a destination. It is a treadmill with a milestone painted on the wall beside it, and the painting is convincing enough that a great many people run their whole lives past it. THAT IS WHERE THIS COURSE ENDS, and it is deliberately not where such courses usually end. Eight lessons of machinery — what investing is, the four risks, what time does, what spreading cannot do, what it costs, what nobody can know, and the steward’s three questions — and the last word is not a technique. It is a destination, because every one of the eight is a means, and a means without a destination will carry a person a very long way from where they meant to go while telling them the whole time that they are making excellent progress.',
